  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=\u27e8(x+2){e}^{y},\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-y}\u27e9$\n- $V(x,y,z)=\u27e85,{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}\u27e9$\n- $W(x,y)=\u27e8x-3{y}^{2},2-y\u27e9$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
